  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(7-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x\\,{|}_{2}^{4}-5\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =7(4-2)+20 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 2+20 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
